# Competitive Metrics — Mega SuperApp vs Major DeFi Platforms

_All figures are drawn from public benchmarks and real testnet or on-chain data as of Q4 2025. Where MegaETH or Mega SuperApp performance is projected, assumptions are based on the verified whitepaper metrics: 100 000+ TPS and sub-10 ms latency._

---

## 1. Core Performance Metrics

| Chain / Platform | Avg TPS | Finality Time | Avg Swap Latency | Architecture Type | Notes |
|------------------|---------|----------------|------------------|------------------|-------|
| **MegaETH (via Mega SuperApp)** | 100 000+ TPS | < 10 ms | 0.01–0.05 s | Real-time, continuous execution | First chain with millisecond-level block finality. Continuous processing instead of block batching. |
| **Solana (Jupiter / Kamino)** | 2 000–5 000 TPS | 400–800 ms | 0.8–1.5 s | Parallel Proof-of-History | Fastest current mainstream L1. Still bound by block intervals and queuing. |
| **Arbitrum (GMX / Gains)** | 300–400 TPS | 2–3 s | 3–6 s | Rollup | Optimistic rollup delay structure adds latency. |
| **Base (Aerodrome)** | 400–500 TPS | 2–4 s | 2–5 s | Rollup | Comparable to Arbitrum, slower due to sequencer congestion. |
| **Ethereum Mainnet (Uniswap)** | 15–20 TPS | 12–15 s | 15–30 s | PoS Layer-1 | Excellent decentralization but poor UX for high-frequency DeFi. |

**Insight**  
Mega SuperApp on MegaETH performs ~200 × faster than Solana-based DeFi and > 1000 × faster than Ethereum-based systems in latency-sensitive operations such as swaps, CLM adjustments, and arbitrage loops.

